<clickhouse>
    <!--
        TESTING topology: a SINGLE ClickHouse node, so it may take a far larger
        share than any one node of the six-node cluster.

        Selected by CH_MEMORY_CONFIG=memory.local.xml (the default). The six-node
        cap lives in memory.xml, and the one-node-per-machine cap for intended
        mode lives in memory.distributed.xml (selected by .env.intended).

        RAISED from 2.5 GB to 3.5 GB when the gold layer moved from Oracle to
        PostgreSQL. The old figure was chosen to "leave room for Oracle (~1.1 GB)"
        alongside MongoDB, Keeper and the pipeline; PostgreSQL holds ~26 MB in the
        same role, so that GB is now free and ClickHouse is the only component
        that can use it.

        The raise is not cosmetic. The widest per-user query — radar_agrifood's
        100-keyword filter, a multiSearchAny plus an arrayExists over the stemmed
        article text — peaks at about 2.5 GB on the 30-day seed, which is exactly
        at the old ceiling. It therefore failed intermittently, and then
        reproducibly, with:

            Code: 241. DB::Exception: Memory limit (total) exceeded:
            would use 2.47 GiB ..., maximum: 2.33 GiB

        (`maximum` reads below the configured cap because the server reserves a
        slice for itself.) Gold was never wrong when this happened — the recompute
        simply aborted and left the previous gold in place — but a recompute that
        cannot finish is not something to leave in the default configuration.

        Measured after the change: the whole testing stack runs at ~2.1 GB total,
        so 3.5 GB for ClickHouse stays inside a 6 GB Docker allocation with room
        for MongoDB, Keeper, PostgreSQL and all five pipeline layers.
    -->
    <max_server_memory_usage>3500000000</max_server_memory_usage>
    <mark_cache_size>268435456</mark_cache_size>
    <uncompressed_cache_size>0</uncompressed_cache_size>
</clickhouse>
